var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"developer_api/#Developer-API","page":"Developer API","text":"The names on this page are versioned extension points for packages that own a feature-map or boundary-handling implementation. They are not intended for ordinary application code. Applications should use the concrete configurations on the public API page and call loss or train!.","title":"Developer API"},{"category":"section","location":"developer_api/#Feature-Maps","page":"Developer API","text":"","title":"Feature Maps"},{"category":"section","location":"developer_api/#Boundary-Handling","page":"Developer API","text":"","title":"Boundary Handling"},{"category":"type","location":"developer_api/#QuantumNLDiffEq.AbstractFeatureMap","page":"Developer API","text":"AbstractFeatureMap\n\nDeveloper extension interface for mappings from a scalar mesh coordinate to the angles of a Differential Quantum Circuit.\n\nRules\n\nPackages that own a feature-map subtype may extend phi, load, and map_to_circuit for that subtype. phi(x, map) must return a scalar angle for every mesh value accepted by the application. load(x, N, map) must return exactly N angles, and map_to_circuit(a, j, map) must return the factor that converts the derivative of phi into the contribution for circuit index j. Extensions must only add methods whose feature-map type they own.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumNLDiffEq.AbstractFeatureMap"},{"category":"function","location":"developer_api/#QuantumNLDiffEq.phi","page":"Developer API","text":"phi(x, map::AbstractFeatureMap)\n\nReturn the scalar feature angle for mesh coordinate x and feature map map.\n\nArguments\n\nx: Scalar mesh coordinate accepted by map.\nmap: Feature-map configuration.\n\nReturns\n\nReturns the angle encoded by one circuit feature gate.\n\nRules\n\nExtensions may define a method only for an AbstractFeatureMap subtype they own. The result must be a scalar accepted by Yao rotation gates for every coordinate accepted by the corresponding training problem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumNLDiffEq.phi"},{"category":"function","location":"developer_api/#QuantumNLDiffEq.load","page":"Developer API","text":"load(x, N, map::AbstractFeatureMap)\n\nReturn the feature angles encoded on the N qubits of a Differential Quantum Circuit at mesh coordinate x.\n\nArguments\n\nx: Scalar mesh coordinate.\nN: Positive number of circuit qubits.\nmap: Feature-map configuration.\n\nReturns\n\nReturns an indexable collection containing exactly N angles.\n\nRules\n\nExtensions may define a method only for an AbstractFeatureMap subtype they own. The collection length must equal N, and every element must be a valid Yao rotation angle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumNLDiffEq.load"},{"category":"function","location":"developer_api/#QuantumNLDiffEq.map_to_circuit","page":"Developer API","text":"map_to_circuit(a, j, map::AbstractFeatureMap)\n\nReturn the feature-map derivative contribution for circuit index j.\n\nArguments\n\na: Derivative of phi at the current mesh coordinate.\nj: Positive circuit index.\nmap: Feature-map configuration.\n\nReturns\n\nReturns the scalar factor multiplying the parameter-shift expectation difference for circuit index j.\n\nRules\n\nExtensions may define a method only for an AbstractFeatureMap subtype they own. The result must preserve the derivative of the angles returned by load for that circuit index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumNLDiffEq.map_to_circuit"},{"category":"type","location":"developer_api/#QuantumNLDiffEq.AbstractBoundaryHandling","page":"Developer API","text":"AbstractBoundaryHandling\n\nDeveloper extension interface for the boundary term used by loss.\n\nRules\n\nPackages that own a subtype may extend calculate_evalue and loss_bound for it. calculate_evalue must return the real-valued state prediction at the requested mesh point. loss_bound must return a nonnegative real penalty and must not mutate the circuit, problem, mesh, or parameter inputs. Extensions must only add methods for boundary types they own.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumNLDiffEq.AbstractBoundaryHandling"},{"category":"function","location":"developer_api/#QuantumNLDiffEq.calculate_evalue","page":"Developer API","text":"calculate_evalue(DQC, cost, u0, boundary, theta, M_value, M_initial)\n\nReturn the state prediction represented by DQC at mesh point M_value.\n\nArguments\n\nDQC: Differential Quantum Circuit whose observable is evaluated.\ncost: Cost-observable blocks for the encoded state component.\nu0: Initial state value for that component.\nboundary: Boundary-handling strategy.\ntheta: Current variational-circuit parameters.\nM_value: Mesh point where the state is evaluated.\nM_initial: First mesh point of the training interval.\n\nReturns\n\nReturns the scalar circuit prediction adjusted according to boundary.\n\nRules\n\nExtensions may define methods only for an AbstractBoundaryHandling subtype they own. A method must return a real-valued state prediction and must not mutate DQC, cost, theta, or either mesh coordinate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumNLDiffEq.calculate_evalue"},{"category":"function","location":"developer_api/#QuantumNLDiffEq.loss_bound","page":"Developer API","text":"loss_bound(DQC, M, u0, cost_params, boundary, loss, theta)\n\nReturn the boundary-condition contribution to the Differential Quantum Circuit training objective.\n\nArguments\n\nDQC: One circuit or a vector of circuits.\nM: First mesh point of the training interval.\nu0: Initial state values.\ncost_params: Cost-observable weights from DQCConfig.\nboundary: Boundary-handling strategy.\nloss: Scalar penalty callable accepting a prediction and target.\ntheta: Current variational-circuit parameters.\n\nReturns\n\nReturns a nonnegative real-valued boundary penalty.\n\nRules\n\nExtensions may define methods only for an AbstractBoundaryHandling subtype they own. Methods must not mutate their inputs and must return zero for strategies that intentionally impose no boundary penalty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumNLDiffEq.loss_bound"},{"category":"section","location":"#QuantumNLDiffEq.jl","page":"Home","text":"QuantumNLDiffEq.jl solves nonlinear differential equations with Differential Quantum Circuits.","title":"QuantumNLDiffEq.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Public-API","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Public API"},{"category":"type","location":"#QuantumNLDiffEq.DQCType","page":"Home","text":"DQCType(; afm, fm, cost, var, N, evol = igate(N))\n\nDescribe a Differential Quantum Circuit (DQC) used by loss and train!.\n\nUse one DQCType for a scalar differential equation, or a vector of DQCTypes for a system where each state component is represented by its own circuit.\n\nFields\n\nafm: Ansatz feature map, such as QuantumNLDiffEq.ChebyshevSparse.\nfm: Feature-map quantum circuit.\ncost: Cost observable or observables. For a single circuit encoding multiple equations, pass a vector of observable vectors.\nvar: Variational quantum circuit.\nN: Number of qubits.\nevol: Optional time-evolution block. Defaults to the identity gate on N qubits.\n\nExamples\n\nusing QuantumNLDiffEq, Yao\n\nDQC = DQCType(\n    afm = QuantumNLDiffEq.ChebyshevSparse(2),\n    fm = chain(6, [put(i => Ry(0)) for i in 1:6]),\n    cost = [Add([put(6, i => Z) for i in 1:6])],\n    var = dispatch(EasyBuild.variational_circuit(6, 5), :random),\n    N = 6,\n)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumNLDiffEq.DQCType"},{"category":"type","location":"#QuantumNLDiffEq.DQCConfig","page":"Home","text":"DQCConfig(; reg = NoRegularisation(), cost_params = NoCostParams(), abh, loss)\n\nConfigure the loss terms and boundary handling used by loss and train!.\n\nDQCConfig keeps the training objective separate from the circuit definition in DQCType. The loss field should be a scalar penalty function that compares a predicted value with a target value.\n\nFields\n\nreg: Regularization data for matching known solution values. Defaults to no regularization.\ncost_params: Weights for cost observables. Defaults to unweighted costs.\nabh: Boundary handling strategy, such as QuantumNLDiffEq.Floating().\nloss: Function called as loss(predicted, target) for each penalty term.\n\nExamples\n\nusing QuantumNLDiffEq\n\nconfig = DQCConfig(\n    abh = QuantumNLDiffEq.Floating(),\n    loss = (predicted, target) -> abs2(predicted - target),\n)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumNLDiffEq.DQCConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"#QuantumNLDiffEq.Product","page":"Home","text":"Product()\n\nFeature map that encodes each mesh coordinate as asin(x) on every qubit.\n\nExamples\n\nusing QuantumNLDiffEq\n\nmap = QuantumNLDiffEq.Product()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumNLDiffEq.Product"},{"category":"type","location":"#QuantumNLDiffEq.ChebyshevSparse","page":"Home","text":"ChebyshevSparse(pc)\n\nFeature map that encodes pc * acos(x) identically on every qubit.\n\nArguments\n\npc: Integer Chebyshev order used to scale the encoded angle.\n\nFields\n\npc: Chebyshev order.\n\nExamples\n\nusing QuantumNLDiffEq\n\nmap = QuantumNLDiffEq.ChebyshevSparse(2)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumNLDiffEq.ChebyshevSparse"},{"category":"type","location":"#QuantumNLDiffEq.ChebyshevTower","page":"Home","text":"ChebyshevTower(pc)\n\nFeature map that encodes the ith qubit with i * pc * acos(x).\n\nArguments\n\npc: Integer Chebyshev order used to scale the encoded angle.\n\nFields\n\npc: Chebyshev order.\n\nExamples\n\nusing QuantumNLDiffEq\n\nmap = QuantumNLDiffEq.ChebyshevTower(2)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumNLDiffEq.ChebyshevTower"},{"category":"type","location":"#QuantumNLDiffEq.Pinned","page":"Home","text":"Pinned(eta = 1.0)\n\nBoundary-handling strategy that penalizes the difference between the circuit prediction and the initial value at the first mesh point.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\neta: Nonnegative multiplier for the boundary penalty. Defaults to 1.0.\n\nFields\n\neta: Boundary-penalty multiplier.\n\nExamples\n\nusing QuantumNLDiffEq\n\nboundary = QuantumNLDiffEq.Pinned(2.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumNLDiffEq.Pinned"},{"category":"type","location":"#QuantumNLDiffEq.Floating","page":"Home","text":"Floating()\n\nBoundary-handling strategy that offsets circuit predictions by the initial state, imposing no separate boundary penalty.\n\nExamples\n\nusing QuantumNLDiffEq\n\nboundary = QuantumNLDiffEq.Floating()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumNLDiffEq.Floating"},{"category":"type","location":"#QuantumNLDiffEq.Optimized","page":"Home","text":"Optimized(fc)\n\nBoundary-handling strategy with a trainable scalar offset.\n\nArguments\n\nfc: Initial scalar offset. train! updates this value while optimizing the circuit parameters.\n\nFields\n\nfc: Current scalar offset.\n\nExamples\n\nusing QuantumNLDiffEq\n\nboundary = QuantumNLDiffEq.Optimized(0.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumNLDiffEq.Optimized"},{"category":"type","location":"#QuantumNLDiffEq.NoRegularisation","page":"Home","text":"NoRegularisation()\n\nConfiguration that disables regularization against known solution values.\n\nExamples\n\nusing QuantumNLDiffEq\n\nreg = QuantumNLDiffEq.NoRegularisation()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumNLDiffEq.NoRegularisation"},{"category":"type","location":"#QuantumNLDiffEq.RegularisationParams","page":"Home","text":"RegularisationParams(u_reg, M_reg, reg_param)\n\nRegularization data that penalizes disagreement with known state values.\n\nArguments\n\nu_reg: Known state values grouped by encoded equation and mesh point.\nM_reg: Mesh points corresponding to each value in u_reg.\nreg_param: Scalar regularization weight used by custom training schedules.\n\nFields\n\nu_reg: Known state values grouped by equation.\nM_reg: Regularization mesh points.\nreg_param: Mutable regularization weight.\n\nExamples\n\nusing QuantumNLDiffEq\n\nreg = QuantumNLDiffEq.RegularisationParams([[1.0, 0.5]], [0.0, 1.0], 0.1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumNLDiffEq.RegularisationParams"},{"category":"type","location":"#QuantumNLDiffEq.NoCostParams","page":"Home","text":"NoCostParams()\n\nConfiguration that uses the cost observables stored in DQCType without additional weights.\n\nExamples\n\nusing QuantumNLDiffEq\n\nweights = QuantumNLDiffEq.NoCostParams()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumNLDiffEq.NoCostParams"},{"category":"type","location":"#QuantumNLDiffEq.CostParams","page":"Home","text":"CostParams(lambda)\n\nWeights for the cost observables used by loss.\n\nArguments\n\nlambda: Observable weights grouped by encoded equation.\n\nFields\n\nlambda: Observable weights grouped by encoded equation.\n\nExamples\n\nusing QuantumNLDiffEq\n\nweights = QuantumNLDiffEq.CostParams([[1.0, 0.5]])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumNLDiffEq.CostParams"},{"category":"function","location":"#QuantumNLDiffEq.loss","page":"Home","text":"loss(DQC, prob, config, M, theta)\n\nEvaluate the training objective for one or more Differential Quantum Circuits.\n\nThe total objective combines the differential-equation residual, the optional regularization penalty configured by config.reg, and the boundary penalty configured by config.abh.\n\nArguments\n\nDQC: A DQCType, or a vector of DQCTypes for systems.\nprob: SciMLBase-compatible ODE problem.\nconfig: DQCConfig containing the scalar penalty function and boundary/regularization settings.\nM: Mesh points where the objective is evaluated.\ntheta: Current variational-circuit parameters.\n\nReturns\n\nReturns the real-valued objective used by train!.\n\nExamples\n\nobjective = loss(DQC, prob, config, M, theta)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumNLDiffEq.loss"},{"category":"function","location":"#QuantumNLDiffEq.train!","page":"Home","text":"train!(DQC, prob, config, M, theta; optimizer = Optimisers.Adam(0.075), steps = 300)\n\nTrain one or more Differential Quantum Circuits against an ODE problem.\n\ntrain! minimizes loss over the mesh points M, updates theta in place, and dispatches the updated parameters back into the variational circuit. It returns nothing.\n\nArguments\n\nDQC: A DQCType, or a vector of DQCTypes for multi-equation systems.\nprob: SciMLBase-compatible ODE problem.\nconfig: Training configuration, including boundary handling and the scalar penalty function.\nM: Mesh points used to evaluate the training objective.\ntheta: Parameters for the variational circuit or circuits. The shape should match DQC: one parameter vector for a single circuit, or one vector per circuit.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\noptimizer: Optimisers.jl optimizer used for gradient updates.\nsteps: Number of training iterations.\n\nExamples\n\nusing SciMLBase, Yao, QuantumNLDiffEq\n\nprob = ODEProblem((u, p, t) -> -p[1] * u * (p[2] + tan(p[1] * t)),\n    [1.0], (0.0, 0.9), [8.0, 0.1])\nDQC = [QuantumNLDiffEq.DQCType(\n    afm = QuantumNLDiffEq.ChebyshevSparse(2),\n    fm = chain(6, [put(i => Ry(0)) for i in 1:6]),\n    cost = [Add([put(6, i => Z) for i in 1:6])],\n    var = dispatch(EasyBuild.variational_circuit(6, 5), :random),\n    N = 6,\n)]\nconfig = DQCConfig(abh = QuantumNLDiffEq.Floating(), loss = (a, b) -> abs2(a - b))\nM = range(0, stop = 0.9, length = 20)\nparams = [Yao.parameters(DQC[1].var)]\n\nQuantumNLDiffEq.train!(DQC, prob, config, M, params)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumNLDiffEq.train!"}]
}
